  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 346 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThrough the day tomorrow, the Lower Ohio Valley remains uneventful \nwith light zonal flow overhead and high pressure in place at the \nsurface from a high centered off the East Coast. To the west, in the \nFour Corners region, an upper low with a nearby surface low is \nsliding slowly to the east, increasing the pressure gradient over \nsouthern Indiana and central Kentucky, driving southwest winds and \nwarm air advection. Winds will ease some, but light WAA will will \nlimit falling temperatures to the 60s tonight before temperatures \nwarm to the upper 80s to low 90s tomorrow as the pressure gradient \ncontinues to increase, causing southwest winds to gust to 25-30 mph. \nWe will see scattered upper level clouds, but low levels remain dry.\n\nThursday night, the aforementioned closed upper low begins to \ndissipate as it gets picked up by stronger zonal flow to the north, \nremaining north of an upper high parked over the Southeast. \nUnderneath this at the surface, a northeast to southwest oriented \ncold front will slide through Indiana and the Ozarks towards the \nCWA. Southern flow ahead of the front will increase precipitable \nwater values from around an inch to closer to two along the front. \n\nAround midnight, rain chances begin to increase from the west and \npush east over the CWA with peak rain chances lasting through the \nday Friday. Model soundings continue to show a very stable column. \nThunder isn't ruled out, but nothing severe is expected. Around half \nof an inch to around an inch is expected across southern Indiana and \ncentral Kentucky. CAA behind the front is expected to drop \ntemperatures into the 70s through the weekend. \n\nBy Saturday morning, rain chances are expected to be south of \ncentral Kentucky before a chance of light rain works back north into \nthe CWA Saturday night. \n\nSunday, an upper trough moving over the Great Lakes pushes a large \nsurface high southeast through the Upper Plains and Midwest, helping \nto push high pressure behind the front into the Ohio Valley,\nbringing an end to rain chances. Mostly clear skies become \ncommon into early next week. The continued CAA is expected to \nlimit highs to the upper 60s for our northern communities on \nMonday and Tuesday before the center of the surface high passes \neast of the CWA, returning WAA and slightly warmer temperatures \nfor midweek.",
